{
  "image_name": "9102cc1a2c65f4ad2787f1cbd50c32be.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure gives a conceptual overview of how organic molecules interact with mineral structures, moving from simple reactants to increasingly complex surface behavior. On the left, the starting materials are shown as mineral structures with pores, aggregates, and particles, together with biomolecule-like organic matter that can undergo oxidative depolymerization into smaller compounds.\n\nThe center emphasizes the bonding mechanisms that attach organics to mineral surfaces. Different mineral surfaces are grouped by charge state: negatively charged, non-charged, and variably charged. The image highlights several interaction types, including cation bridging and exchange involving ions such as Ca²⁺ and K⁺, hydrogen bonding, van der Waals forces, hydrophobic interactions, and ligand exchange. These interactions appear to determine how molecules sorb onto distinct mineral surfaces.\n\nOn the right, the figure summarizes the broader consequences of these surface associations. Adsorbed molecules can partition out of solution and assemble into multilayer structures on minerals, although their exact organization seems uncertain. Sorbed compounds also compete with one another and with inorganic ions, can alter degradation rates and persistence, and may later desorb from surfaces, sometimes with conformational changes. Overall, the figure presents mineral–organic associations as diverse, dynamic, and still only partly understood.",
